{"question": "A 3-year-old male is brought in to his pediatrician by his mother because she is concerned that he is not growing appropriately. Physical examination is notable for frontal bossing and shortened upper and lower extremities. His axial skeleton appears normal. He is at the 7th percentile for height and 95th percentile for head circumference. He demonstrates normal intelligence and is able to speak in three-word sentences. He first sat up without support at twelve months and started walking at 24 months. Genetic analysis reveals an activating mutation in a growth factor receptor. Which of the following physiologic processes is most likely disrupted in this patient\u2019s condition?", "options": {"A": "Intramembranous ossification", "B": "Osteoblast maturation", "C": "Endochondral ossification", "D": "Production of type I collagen", "E": "Bone resorption"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Genetics", "Endocrinology", "Orthopedics", "Neurodevelopmental Disorders"], "option_domains": ["Skeletal Development", "Bone Growth Processes"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The scenario described suggests a condition known as **Pseudohypoparathyroidism Type 1a (PHP1a)**, which is a genetic disorder characterized by resistance to parathyroid hormone (PTH) and often associated with specific physical features and developmental delays. However, the presence of an activating mutation in a growth factor receptor, along with the physical examination findings and developmental history, points more towards **Fibrous Dysplasia/McCune-Albright Syndrome (MAS)**. ### Key Features of the Condition:\n1. **Frontal Bossing**: This is a characteristic feature of McCune-Albright Syndrome (MAS), where there is an abnormal overgrowth of bone, leading to a prominent forehead. 2. **Shortened Upper and Lower Extremities**: This can be indicative of bone dysplasia, which is common in MAS. 3. **Normal Axial Skeleton**: This is consistent with MA